# 9. PREPARE A DOCUMENT LIBRARY USING CLOUDFLARE R2

Extend the existing application architecture to support a library of business documents.

Examples include:

* Startup guides.
* Business planning templates.
* Budget worksheets.
* Market research guides.
* Marketing resources.
* Record-keeping templates.
* Educational PDFs.
* Other business development materials.

Do not bundle a large document collection inside the Android application.

### Planned storage architecture

Use Cloudflare R2 as the intended object storage for remotely hosted documents.

The future architecture should support:

Android application
↓
Django REST API or equivalent secured service
↓
Document metadata and access control
↓
Cloudflare R2
↓
PDFs, templates, images, and other files

The actual files belong in R2. Metadata belongs in PostgreSQL on the server and SQLite locally where appropriate.

Document metadata should support:

* Stable document ID.
* Title and description.
* Category.
* File type and size.
* Language.
* Version.
* Free or premium classification.
* Publication status.
* Last-updated date.
* Remote object key.
* Download status.
* Local file location.

### Offline document access

Once a user downloads a document, it should remain accessible without internet.

Use suitable persistent application storage for downloaded files. Store document metadata in the local database.

Handle incomplete downloads, file replacement, version updates, and file removal safely.

### Security

* Never embed Cloudflare secret keys or R2 credentials in the Android app.
* Do not expose private bucket credentials.
* Use a properly secured backend or equivalent authorization mechanism for restricted downloads.
* Use expiring signed URLs or appropriate access controls where needed.
* Do not treat an obscure URL as a security mechanism.
* Separate public documents from restricted or premium resources.

For the current implementation, build the document model, storage abstraction, and local document handling only where appropriate.

Do not introduce an unnecessary backend dependency into the core application.

If actual Cloudflare credentials are unavailable, prepare the integration cleanly and document the remaining setup. Do not pretend remote uploads or downloads are operational when they have not been configured and tested.

